<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#karmapa">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karmapa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karmapa is the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> and the oldest line of reincarnated lamas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, beginning with Düsum Khyenpa in the 12th century. The Karmapas were patrons and allies of the Rinpungpa and Tsangpa rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> in the 15th to 17th centuries, rivals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karmapa.jpg" alt="Karmapa" width="266" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: an employee of the Austrian Buddhist society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karmapa16_3_gross.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Karmapa tulku lineage is the oldest among the major incarnating lineages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. It was established in 1110 CE by the first Karmapa, Düsum Khyenpa, a disciple of the Tibetan master Gampopa. The title means the one who carries out buddha-activity. Düsum Khyenpa is said to have attained enlightenment at the age of fifty while practising dream yoga, and the masters Shakya Śri and Lama Shang regarded him as the Karmapa, a manifestation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avalokitesvara">Avalokiteśvara</a> whose coming believers hold to have been foretold in the Samadhiraja Sutra and the Laṅkāvatāra Sūtra.</p><p>The Karmapa heads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a>, the largest sub-school of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu school</a>, one of the four major schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. Its oral lineage is traditionally traced back to the Buddha Vajradhara, through Tilopa, Naropa, Marpa Lotsawa and Milarepa, forefathers of the Kagyu collectively called the Golden Rosary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The second Karmapa, Karma Pakshi (1204 to 1283), was the first recognised tulku in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a> to predict the circumstances of his own rebirth. A Karmapa's identity is confirmed through the insight of realised lineage teachers, prediction letters left by the previous Karmapa, and the young child's own declaration and ability to identify objects and people known to the previous incarnation. The 8th, 10th, 12th and 16th Karmapas each faced conflicts over their recognition, which were eventually resolved.</p><p>The Karmapas hold the Black Crown, which tradition says was woven by dakinis from their hair; they are sometimes called the Black Hat Lamas. The physical crown was offered to the fifth Karmapa, Deshin Shekpa, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The main seat of the lineage is Tsurphu Monastery in U-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and the seat built during the Tibetan diaspora is Rumtek Monastery in Sikkim, where the 16th Karmapa, Rangjung Rigpe Dorje (1924 to 1981), spent his last years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Seventeen Karmapas have been recognised. After the 16th died in 1981, Orgyen Trinley Dorje, born in 1985, was located and recognised in 1992 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> and by the Chinese Central Government. Trinley Thaye Dorje, born in 1983, was recognised by Shamar Rinpoche, and the enthronement of two 17th Karmapas became a controversy. Both men issued a joint statement on 4 December 2023. The lineage has international monastic seats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica">Dominica</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karmapa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a journey begun in 1403, Deshin Shekpa, the 5th <b>Karmapa</b> and head of the Karma Kagyu school of Tibetan Buddhism, arrived at the Ming capital Nanjing riding an elephant.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-10">10 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They were patrons of the Karma Kagyu school and its <b>Karmapa</b> lamas, and under Donyö Dorje, at the height of their power around 1500, they took control of the Lhasa region and banned Gelug monks from Lhasa's great prayer festival between 1498 and 1517.</blockquote><a href="/state/rinpungpa">Rinpungpa</a></li></ul></section>
